xtÙ+And despise not thy mother when she is old.¦layout§quote_2ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines’‚¤text¿Buy the truth, and sell it not;¦layout ‚¤textÙ0Also wisdom, and instruction, and understanding.¦layout§quote_2ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines’‚¤textÙ2The father of the righteous shall greatly rejoice:¦layout ‚¤textÙ9And he that begetteth a wise child shall have joy of him.¦layout§quote_2ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines’‚¤textÙ(Thy father and thy mother shall be glad,¦layout ‚¤textÙ%And she that bare thee shall rejoice.¦layout§quote_2ªlast_verse…¤book§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses“„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines’‚¤text¼My son, give me thine heart,¦layout ‚¤textÙ#And let thine eyes observe my ways.¦layout§quote_2ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines’‚¤text¼For a whore is a deep ditch;¦layout ‚¤textÙ$And a strange woman is a narrow pit.¦layout§quote_2ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines’‚¤textÙ%She also lieth in wait as for a prey,¦layout ‚¤textÙ+And increaseth the transgressors among men.¦layout§quote_2ªlast_verse…¤book§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse#¦verses—„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines”‚¤text¾Who hath woe? Who hath sorrow?¦layout ‚¤textÙ(Who hath contentions? Who hath babbling?¦layout§quote_2‚¤text¾Who hath wounds without cause?¦layout§quote_1‚¤text¹Who hath redness of eyes?¦layout§quote_2ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines’‚¤textÙ!They that tarry long at the wine;¦layout ‚¤textÙ They that go to seek mixed wine.¦layout§quote_2ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines“‚¤textÙ+Look not thou upon the wine when it is red,¦layout ‚¤textÙ%When it giveth his colour in the cup,¦layout§quote_2‚¤text½When it moveth itself aright.¦layout§quote_2ªlast_verse„¥verse ¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines’‚¤textÙ%At the last it biteth like a serpent,¦layout ‚¤text»And stingeth like an adder.¦layout§quote_2ªlast_verse „¥verse!¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines’‚¤textÙ&Thine eyes shall behold strange women,¦layout ‚¤textÙ,And thine heart shall utter perverse things.¦layout§quote_2ªlast_verse!„¥verse"¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines’‚¤textÙAYea, thou shalt be as he that lieth down in the midst of the sea,¦layout ‚¤textÙ+Or as he that lieth upon the top of a mast.¦layout§quote_2ªlast_verse"„¥verse#¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines”‚¤textÙ:They have stricken me, shalt thou say, and I was not sick;¦layout ‚¤textÙ'They have beaten me, and I felt it not:¦layout§quote_2‚¤text³When shall I awake?¦layout§quote_1‚¤text¹I will seek it yet again.¦layout§quote_2ªlast_verse#